From 650cdd0dd7e992ee76665097c0fb148e393586b7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 01:48:18 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Mean thermodynamic forces: sums computed by kernels

`computeMeanThermodynamicForcesValues` read the thermodynamic forces on the
host and summed them there, point by point: on a GPU, the thermodynamic
forces (or their values in the global frame, 9 components per point for an
orthotropic behaviour in finite strain) were copied to the host after each
resolution, and summed by one thread.

When the post-processings are computed by kernels (environment variable
`MFEM_MGIS_POST_PROCESSINGS_COMPUTATION`, or post-processings placed on the
GPU by the option `--execution`), the sums are now reductions over the
integration points, one per component, launched where the kernels run
(`NonLinearEvolutionProblemImplementationBase::
addThermodynamicForcesIntegralsByKernels`, `batch::addWeightedSums`): the
thermodynamic forces are neither synchronized on the host nor copied to
it. The weights are the ones kept by the problem, copied once for the
kernels; the measure is their sum, computed once on the host in the order
of the points, as the loop of the host does. The vector of the
thermodynamic forces in the global frame and the workspace of the
reductions are kept between the calls.

The contributions are accumulated in double, in an order which depends on
the device: the integrals are the ones of the loop of the host up to
round-off errors. Without kernels for the post-processings (the default),
the computation and its results are unchanged. One reduction per component
being launched for each material, the computation by kernels is meant for a
few materials with many points.

IntegrationPointsWeightsTest: integrals computed element by element then by
kernels (counter of the post-processings computed by kernels, same
integrals up to round-off errors, same volumes), for an isotropic behaviour
and for an orthotropic one with rotated material frames, on the host and on
the debug device of MFEM.
